  "query_preview": "..\n\nWhere there was clearing this morning showers and thunderstorms\nhave developed. Substantial moisture remains in place with \nsatellite derived PWATs around 1.3\" to 1.7\", higher values Tucson\nwestward and surface dewpoints in the upper 50s to low 60s. \nThe Weather Prediction Center (WPC) has areas Tucson eastward \nin Slight risk for excessive rainfall. The 12Z HREF highlights \nthis area with a 10-50% chance for greater than 1 inch of \nrainfall, in a 3 hour period, with pockets of up to 2 inches \npossible. While there is suitable instability in place, the main\nconcern today is flash flooding. There is a chance a storm or\ntwo could produce gusty to strong outflow winds with small hail.\nRainfall overnight and over the past several days has made the \narea highly susceptible for flash flooding thus any more rain \nthat falls will have a much higher chance to cause impacts. \nTemperatures today will be 5 to 7 degrees below normal.\n\nDry air begins to move into the area beginning tomorrow moving\nin across the area through weekend. Enough moisture will be in\nplace for daily chances for afternoon into the evening showers\nand thunderstorms though they will mainly be to the south and\neast of Tucson. During this time temperatures begin to gradually\nrise eventually pushing high temperatures 5 to 7 degrees above\nnormal. This will bring HeatRisk to Major for the lower deserts\nand Extreme for some locales including Tucson and Safford by\nMonday. Due to this, an Extreme Heat Watch has been issued \nstarting Monday through Wednesday for a majority of Pima county,\nPinal county, and southern Graham and Greenlee counties.\nTemperatures begin to cool Thursday and chances for daily\nthunderstorms begin to increase.",
